Kindling is very usable as a donor/reference base, but we should not copy its architecture wholesale because its core persistence model is exactly what we decided LithoMark should avoid: Kindling stores project data in SQLite, and many of its Rust commands and frontend stores are built around that database layer. GitHub
The good news is that the UI, editor, workflow, and parser layers are much more reusable than the persistence layer.
Here is the practical map.
Strong candidates to reuse or port
src/lib/components/Sidebar.svelte
Good donor for chapter/scene navigation behavior, collapse/expand logic, selection handling, ordering, and writer-oriented hierarchy. We should port the behavior and presentation, but wire it to LithoMark’s folder tree instead of database records. Kindling explicitly uses this component as its chapter/scene tree. GitHub
src/lib/components/ScenePanel.svelte
Very important donor. This is Kindling’s main writing area and handles beats + prose workflow. The underlying UX idea is valuable for LithoMark’s Manuscript view. We should keep the editing patterns but replace database reads/writes with Markdown-file loading and saving. GitHub
src/lib/components/ReferencesPanel.svelte
Very useful for LithoMark’s Story Bible/context panel. Kindling already supports characters, locations, items, objectives, and organizations in this panel. We can port the panel concept and adapt it to LithoMark nodes/files. GitHub
src/lib/components/StartScreen.svelte
Good reference for project creation/opening/import workflow. We can adapt it to LithoMark’s:
- New Project
- Open Local Project
- Clone GitHub Repository
instead of Kindling’s database-backed project library. GitHub
src/lib/components/ArchivePanel.svelte
Potentially useful later for archived scenes/chapters, though this is not a first-pass priority. GitHub
src/lib/stores/ui.svelte.ts
Good architectural pattern for UI-only state like:
- active view
- collapsed panels
- focus mode
- onboarding
- modal state
This kind of store is safe to reuse conceptually because it is not tied to SQLite. GitHub
TipTap 3 editor stack
Kindling uses TipTap 3 for rich-text editing, with underline, alignment, starter kit, etc. That is worth serious consideration for LithoMark’s manuscript editor because it is already proven in Kindling’s writing workflow. GitHub
Kindling’s import parsers
This is a very valuable donor area. Kindling already has native Rust parsers for:
- Plottr
- Scrivener
- yWriter
- novelWriter
- Longform/Obsidian
- Markdown
Those parsers currently feed a ParsedProject into SQLite, but the parsing itself can be adapted to emit LithoMark folders/files instead. GitHub
Export logic
Kindling already exports DOCX, EPUB, Markdown, Longform, Scrivener, novelWriter, HTML, text, and treatments. That is a strong donor subsystem later. GitHub
Revision/editorial review concepts
Kindling already has named drafts, compare/restore, comments, tracked suggestions, review packages, status tracking, etc. These are excellent later-stage features. We should port them only after we decide how LithoMark stores revisions as files rather than database rows. GitHub
Useful conceptually, but needs major rewrite
src/lib/stores/project.svelte.ts
This is Kindling’s main project state. It exposes chapters, scenes, beats, characters, locations, etc., but it assumes the Rust backend is returning database-backed records. GitHub
For LithoMark we should keep the idea of a central project store, but redefine it around:
Project root folder
File tree
Parsed frontmatter
Current file
Entity index
Links/backlinks
Workspace state
Git state

rather than database IDs.
So conceptually useful, structurally incompatible.
src-tauri/src/commands/
Kindling has a large command layer for CRUD, reorder, sync, archive, export, snapshot, settings, etc. There are 166 Tauri commands now. GitHub
The command-boundary pattern is good. The actual commands are mostly tied to SQLite and need to be rewritten around filesystem operations.
For example:
Kindling:
get_chapters(projectId)
create_chapter(...)
delete_scene(...)

LithoMark:
scan_project(rootPath)
read_markdown(path)
write_markdown(path, content)
create_node(type, path, metadata)
rename_node(oldPath, newPath)
move_node(...)

Same pattern, different persistence.
src-tauri/src/models/
Kindling’s models are good references for concepts like Project, Chapter, Scene, Beat, Character, Location, ReferenceItem. But those models map directly to database tables today. GitHub
We should derive LithoMark’s own file-backed node model instead.
Do not reuse as architecture
src-tauri/src/db/
This is the part we should leave behind completely.
Kindling’s schema, migrations, queries, and database ownership are the opposite of the storage philosophy we just locked in. GitHub
For LithoMark, there should be no equivalent authoritative database layer.
At most we might someday have:
.lithomark/cache/

for disposable indexes, but it must always be rebuildable.
Also do not reuse Kindling branding/design system
Kindling’s current UI is built around its Press design system, which supplies colors, typography, controls, themes, and brand artwork. GitHub
We should not carry that over.
LithoMark gets its own:
- #242424 primary background
- gold accent family
- muted blue interactions
- off-white text
- its own typography
- its own icons
- its own component tokens
So structurally:
Kindling Press
→ discard

LithoMark Design System
→ build fresh

What Kindling already gives us that overlaps Plottr
Kindling already has:
- scene/chapter organization
- drag/drop outline behavior
- beat structures
- story-structure templates
- Plottr import
- scene synopsis
- references
- tags/custom fields
- revision statuses
- rolling outline
So we do not need Novalist for basic outlining or beat planning.
Where Novalist remains valuable is the more visual plotting layer:
Plot Grid
Plotline × Scene matrix
Timeline
Corkboard / planning board
Relationship graph
Series planning
Custom wiki/entity views

That is still a real gap.
My recommendation after this code audit
The best engineering path now is:
LithoMark
│
├── Kindling-derived
│   ├── app shell patterns
│   ├── writing editor workflow
│   ├── sidebar/manuscript navigation
│   ├── beats/outlining
│   ├── references/context panel
│   ├── import parsers
│   ├── export system
│   └── revision concepts
│
├── Novalist-derived
│   ├── plot grid
│   ├── timeline
│   ├── corkboard
│   ├── graph
│   └── richer wiki/codex UX
│
└── LithoMark-original
    ├── filesystem-first project model
    ├── Markdown/YAML node model
    ├── backlinks/wiki links
    ├── Git/GitHub tab
    ├── integrated terminal
    ├── repository management
    └── visual identity

The most important conclusion from the code-level check is this:
Kindling’s frontend/editor/workflow code is valuable. Kindling’s database architecture is not.
That means we should not fork Kindling and then try to remove SQLite later. We should treat Kindling as a donor source and port the useful pieces into LithoMark’s filesystem-first architecture from the beginning.